Here's a bit of a neat YANI I thought up - nothing serious, just for
fun. At the moment, we have a whole bunch of conducts, but they're
rather arbitrary; there's no justification given for *why* you would
venture into the dungeons blindfold, or refuse to eat any solid food,
or vow to make every type of monster extinct.

It would be kind of neat if there was a bit of a setup story that
*explained* why you were doing this, at the beginning of the game. For
example, after the player chooses a name, class, and gender, the
following screen would appear:

Conducts?:
- Extinctionist
- Atheist
- Pacifist
- Illiterate
- Naked
- Blind
- Foodless

to name just a few of the common ones. (You could, of course, choose
None). Then after the normal intro screens appear, the following would
come up (suppose you chose "Pacifist":)

"As well as being a Wizard [or whatever], you belong to the Holy Order
of the White Dove. As a member of this most secret and solemn order,
you have sworn never to shed the blood of another living creature. You
have taken a vow to retrieve the Amulet from the depths of the
Dungeons of Doom while adhering to this condition, or to be cast out
of the Order forever! Accordingly, you have entered the dungeon with
no weapon but a toothpick [or something similarly silly.] The best of
luck to you on your quest!" Your starting inventory would be adjusted
accordingly.

Or suppose you chose "Illiterate":

"You, Barbarian, belong to the Brotherhood of the Primitives, an
ancient society that rejects all forms of learning. Accordingly, you
can neither read nor write, and regard such activities as most
despicable and contemptuous. You have sworn an oath in blood to
descend to the deepest depths of the dungeon and bring back the
Amulet, and in so doing to prove to all the world that literacy is
utterly superfluous!" (You would then not be able to read or write
throughout the game - all scrolls would appear either "blank", or
meaningless. Spellbooks would be identified by color only.)

Or, for a really silly one, "Foodless":

"You, Ranger, are a member of the Ethereal Circle of Light. Along with
your friends in this most sacred society, you have sworn never again
to let a bite of food pass your lips, so that you may purge yourself
of all earthly dross and ascend to the highest heavens. You have sworn
to adhere to this stringent condition, consuming liquid only, as you
pass through the Dungeons of Doom and retrieve the Amulet. Touch even
the smallest portion of food, and you will be shamed forevermore!"
(Again, your starting inventory would be adjusted to include no food
(and perhaps a potion of water instead.))

As you progressed through the dungeons, you would of course meet other
people, some of whom would now belong to your conduct group and whom
would have a higher chance of being friendly. On the other hand, any
other beings *opposed* to your conduct (i.e. a Pacifist meeting a
soldier, who is pretty well an extinctionist) would be hostile. Your
abilities would be adjusted to account for the conduct, if required
(e.g. an illiterate person would be completely unable to read, a blind
person completely unable to see, an atheist would have no alignment at
all and therefore prayer would have no effect, a pacifist would be
unable to progress in any weapon skill set except pickaxe, and so
forth.) Also, there could be a "Home Level" for the conduct similar to
the Quest Level, with possibly a mini-quest that you have to solve.
You would be rewarded with a unique item that would help you maintain
your conduct (e.g. a pacifist with a figurine of a very strong
defensive pet, an armorless player with a +7 ring of protection or
something similar, and so forth.) At the end of your game, your Order
(if you managed to successfully keep it!) would be displayed in your
attributes:

e.g.
You were fire-resistant.
You were cold-resistant.
You were the Envoy of Balance.
You were a faithful member of the Ethereal Circle of Light.

If you had broken the conduct, this would be mentioned as well:

You were once a member of the Ethereal Circle of Light, but after
shamefully yielding to temptation you were irrevocably cast out of the
society. (Points would also be deducted, or added for a
successfully-kept conduct.)

Any thoughts?

